# The cram tests that are only sometimes enabled, and the environment
# variable that enables each one: the `.t` file, relative to the repository
# root, then the variable the stanza's `(enabled_if ...)` reads.
#
# `scripts/runtest.sh` fails the test step when a `.t` file in this tree has
# no `cram.out` record after a green run, which is how the saved output of a
# run shows that the cram tests executed instead of being served from a warm
# `_build`. A test whose stanza is switched off writes no record either, so
# a test that is only sometimes enabled cannot be told from a test that did
# not run. It says which it is here: the runner then demands the record when
# the variable is on, and reports the test as not executed, with the reason,
# when it is off. A `.t` file that is not listed here is always demanded, so
# this file can never excuse one.
#
# The condition is dune's own. A stanza written
#
#   (enabled_if (<> %{env:VAR=none} "none"))
#
# compares `%{env:VAR=none}` with the word `none`, and dune substitutes that
# word when VAR is unset. The condition is therefore off when VAR is unset or
# holds the word `none`, and on otherwise — including when it holds the empty
# string, which dune substitutes as itself. The runner reads the variable the
# same way, so the two rules cannot disagree.
#
# Adding a line here enables nothing: the stanza decides that. The line says
# what to expect from the run, and the runner checks it against the `.t`
# file, against the tree, and against an `(enabled_if ...)` naming that
# variable in the `dune` file beside the test.

test/context/entry.t JASMIN_CONTEXT_CORPUS
test/context/jinc-alone.t JASMIN_CONTEXT_CORPUS
test/context/jinc-behind-entry.t JASMIN_CONTEXT_CORPUS
test/context/jinc-in-context.t JASMIN_CONTEXT_CORPUS
test/context/required-file.t JASMIN_CONTEXT_CORPUS
